A||||16||Gold-86SE||Adding Cruise Control - What's Needed?||||5||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||10-02-2001||09:38 AM||||<BR>Ok, I have an 84 SE 4.10 4-speed and I also have an 84 Automatic parts car.  My idea is to take the cruise control from the Automatic and put it in the 84 4-speed.<P>So far, I have pulled the cruise control, the vacuum canister (coffee can) and wiring harness (that goes to the cruise) from the engine compartement of the Automatic.<P>What I need to understand is what do I need to pull from the interior.  The turn signal stalk and what else?<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>84 SE Automatic (Parts Car)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC Automatic (Blown Engine, seeking 2.5)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-02-2001||11:00 AM||||You'll have to figure out the wiring from the steering column back to the engine compartment.  I would get an aftermrket kit...I'm thinking about it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||10-02-2001||11:17 AM||||Didn't The Ogre do some research on this a while back.  Try a search and then maybe contact him.   ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||10-02-2001||02:30 PM||||<BR>fyi, and I am not sure if it makes a difference, however, I did a search and really did not find an anything to sufficiently answer my question regarding what stuff I need to pull from the Fiero interior to add cruise.<P>Like Hank said, maybe Ogre could help. Ogre?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||10-02-2001||03:15 PM||||<BR>From what I have read, when using the search function, is:<BR>1) that the turn signal stalk can be worked with out opening the column - this is good news<P>2) there is a clutch cancel switch associated with a standard Fiero, located near the clutch pedal<P><BR>What I need to know:<BR>1) For an 84 4-speed, is there a special wire harness that includes the cruise control?  The engine bay has to have the cruise control wiring harness, but does the interior have something similiar?<P>2) Does the cruise on an 84 use the ECM in any way?  In essence, will I need a new ECM?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||10-02-2001||07:59 PM||||I did this same conversion on an 84 about 10 years ago.  The main wire harness inside the car is different.  The cruise control wiring doesn't exist in the non cruise car.  I ended up just changing out the entire harness rather than try to run seperate wiring.  The speedometer will also have to be swapped since the 84 has all the cruise control electronics on the speedometer circuit board.  The ecm on the 84's doesn't have anything to do with cruise, so you can leave it alone.  It's not an impossible job by any means, but it's not something you will do in an afternoon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-02-2001||08:21 PM||||What Rod said...<P>only 87-88 4 cyl have the cruise integrated into the ECM.<P>Definatly not a one day project to migrate a factory setup. Easier, and likely safer, for most people to just get the aftermarket system for it. Several people here have done that and are very happy with it.<P>Even if you are doing a show setup,,, depending on show class, the RPO codes will give away to the judges that you don't have OE cruise.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||10-02-2001||11:00 PM||||I did some research on this one as well, and didnt get very far into taking my 87 V6 apart before I threw down the 89.99 on the Audiovox kit and installed it.  It works like a champ and you can wire it to the OEM controls on the signal wand so from the interior it looks stock.  <P>Ogre's said it before and Ill say it again.  In the case of the cruise control, it definitley pays to get the new hardware.  It looks really close to stock and performs well.  You could spend a long time troubleshooting the vacuum problems or electrical problems from a system removed from another car.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> <BR>"And this one time, on the Fiero Forum..."||||reg||1
Z||000008||Max The Chainsaw||10-03-2001||07:04 AM||||AS for what has to be removed, there is a cruise control module inside the car. It is located on the drivers side of the center console behind the carpeting and the plastic side panel, down by the gas pedal. It is a black box about 5 inches square and an inch thick.<P>You will definatly need that as well.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||10-03-2001||08:57 AM||||DJRice;  Where did you buy the Audiovox unit? Any PN? TIA||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gold-86SE||10-03-2001||09:14 AM||||<BR>Thanks guys for the input and advice.  Yeah, I realize the RPO code would not reference any cruise that I might add, especially if it is a factory cruise, but to me that is ok.<P>I am really debating how much time, effort and parts are needed to do a factory install of cruise, since I have the parts, well except for the clutch disengage switch versus not doing adding cruise.  The task does seem to be quite involved.<P>For curiousity's sake, DJRice, how much effort is needed to put an Audiovox cruise in the Fiero?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||10-03-2001||11:41 PM||||The audiovox unit can be purchased thru JCWhitney, as well as Discount Auto Parts, Pep Boys, and Autozone.  I checked all the local parts stores and found that Autozone was selling it for $89.99 where the other two were asking $99.99.  The part number is CCS-100 and can be installed by anyone with some shadetree mechanic experience in about a half a day.  The upgrade to the OEM Control Stick instead of the included control pad tacks on another couple of hours.<P>Its basically only about 6 wires to install the thing:  +12 Constant, +12 Ignition, Ground, VSS, Brake Input, and the 4 wire control pad.  You can mount the servo in the same location as the factory one would be and then T-off the vacuum port on the intake plenum.  I didnt spring for the accumulator and have had no problems.  <P>I will charge up my digital camera batteries and snap a few pics tomorrow.  For the time and money, you cant beat it.  I think its the best money ive spent on my car yet with the exception of the obvious stuff like tires, brakes, and the cd player.<P>--Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||10-04-2001||08:37 AM||||Thanks DJ.  Yes please. Pictures would be great. TIA||||reg||1
Z||000013||Gold-86SE||10-04-2001||09:10 AM||||<BR>Alex beat me to it ;-) but yes I would like to see the pictures too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Rodrv6||10-04-2001||02:47 PM||||Max- the 84's didn't use the module you refer to.  All of the electronics for the cruise were integrated into the speedometer circuit board.  HOWEVER, if the car was taken to a dealer for a cruise control repair it's possible the box may have been installed as part of a repair kit.  There was a service bulletin about adding the module to the 84's if needed.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||10-04-2001||11:06 PM||||Here are the pics I promised....<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djrwand.jpg"> <BR>This is a Cruise/Delay wiper wand I purchased from Rodney Dickman.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djrservo.jpg"> <BR>The servo and electronics are all contained in one convienient package.<P>This cruise works like a champ!  I recommend it to anyone thinking of adding a cruise system.<P>--Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000016||Gold-86SE||10-05-2001||11:23 AM||||<BR>DJ, thanks for the pics and thanks for the input guys.||||reg||1
